call_details.response:
The board state has no immediate winning move for o (Player o). Exhaustive examination of all legal drop positions (columns 0, 1, 4, 5 and 6) shows that none produces four consecutive o discs.

The same examination reveals exactly three cells that complete four-in-a-row for the side that occupies them:

- (0,4) – winning for o (diagonal through the already-present discs at (1,3), (2,2) and (3,1));
- (0,1) – winning for x;
- (4,5) – winning for x.

None of these three cells is currently the lowest empty cell of its column, so none is an immediate threat. They become threats only when the cell beneath them is filled:

- filling column 4 makes (0,4) playable;
- filling column 1 makes (0,1) playable;
- filling column 5 makes (4,5) playable.

Consequently the only move that creates an immediate threat for o is column 4 (o drops at (1,4)). After that drop (0,4) is the lowest empty cell of column 4 and is a win for o, forcing x to answer in column 4. All other legal columns either give x an immediate win on the following turn (columns 1 and 5) or create no threat at all (columns 0 and 6).

By seizing the single available threat o obtains the initiative. The forced reply by x also places a disc high in column 4 that cannot participate in any further vertical four for either side, while o’s own disc at (1,4) participates in several later-forcing diagonals once the right-hand side of the board is developed. Because the reply is forced, o retains the freedom on the subsequent turn to begin the only remaining pure-vertical battle (column 6) or to prepare additional double-threat patterns that x cannot answer.

Thus column 4 is the unique optimal opening move.

Final Answer: 4

